  "slug": "quantum-adapted-fddb",
  "summary": "New Trotter variants, classical fermionic and spin-bath simulators, and a sampling-efficient energy-difference algorithm sharpen quantum simulation tooling.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is reducing the cost of simulating quantum dynamics, on quantum hardware and classically. On the circuit side, one preprint schedules sub-Hamiltonians in Trotter decomposition by combining simultaneous diagonalization with higher-order Trotter-Suzuki ideas. Another builds an integrable Lie-Trotter-Suzuki decomposition for unitary integration of nonlinear dynamics, applied to the Stepanoff flow. An early-FTQC algorithm estimates small energy differences such as molecular forces directly from dynamics, with lower sampling cost than phase estimation. Classical counterparts are also prominent. Dirac propagation simulates local fermionic observables under particle-number-conserving unitaries. Cluster spin-LSC treats a sensor and strongly coupled bath spins quantum mechanically to model spin-qubit decoherence. A polynomial-cost effective Hamiltonian method models couplings in 96-transmon processors and predicts 100-qubit quench dynamics. Separately, a hybrid framework uses simulated annealing and quantum-assisted ground-state estimation to find compact bases for ab initio many-fermion problems. Explicit symmetry adaptation is less central than in earlier work; particle-number conservation in Dirac propagation is the closest case.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
